  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 371fcced5420eda46b9cb39fda4431ce, type: 3}
  m_Name: Amaryllis
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::FlowerData
  id: amaryllis
  displayName: Amaryllis
  scientificName: "Hippeastrum \xD7 hybridum"
  markerImageName: amaryllis
  description: Amaryllis is a tender bulbous plant grown for its large, trumpet-shaped
    flowers. Each thick, upright flower stalk can carry several blooms in shades
    of red, pink, white, orange or striped combinations. Long, strap-shaped green
    leaves usually appear during or after flowering. Although commonly called amaryllis,
    most ornamental indoor plants belong to the genus Hippeastrum.
  origin: Cultivated hybrids derived from species native to tropical and subtropical
    regions of the Americas, especially South America
  meaning: Strength, confidence, determination, pride and radiant beauty. Its tall
    stems and dramatic flowers are often associated with success and personal achievement.
  habitat: Warm tropical and subtropical environments, including seasonally dry forests,
    grasslands and rocky slopes. Cultivated varieties are usually grown indoors in
    containers.
  birthflower: Not an official traditional birth flower; commonly associated with
    winter and the Christmas season
  floweringSeason: December to March when grown indoors; bulbs usually flower six
    to eight weeks after planting
  sunlight: Bright, indirect light or gentle direct morning sunlight. Keep the plant
    in a well-lit location and rotate the pot regularly so the flower stalk grows
    upright.
  watering: Water very sparingly after planting until new growth appears. Once leaves
    and the flower stalk begin growing, water regularly when the top layer of soil
    feels dry. Do not allow the pot to stand in water, and avoid pouring water directly
    onto the bulb.
  difficulty: Easy to Moderate
  flowerImage: {fileID: 0}
  flowerPrefab: {fileID: 919132149155446097, guid: a1832e70e2829f54086630d62f31a90b, type: 3}
